{
  "id": "public-multihistory-feedback",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Two-to-six-rune feedback and autokey screen",
  "question": "Do seven fixed multi-rune history functions under ciphertext, plaintext or mixed feedback yield an English-like LP2 reading in the published finite grid?",
  "reader_question": "Could each rune be decoded using a key derived from the preceding runes?",
  "summary": "A pinned public 23,520-configuration feedback sweep is rerun against LP2, with 21 planted controls and an independently recomputed winning decode.",
  "finding": "The source grid best is -6.299 on a 66-rune segment, below the -5.500 advancement bar. All 21 planted k=3 controls recover correctly; no tested output provides a convincing English reading.",
  "limitations": "Plaintext and mixed history used only 29 all-equal initial states out of 29^k possibilities. Their wrong seeds need not converge. The 200 raw-stream shuffles are not a best-of-23,520 null and the best candidate is a short segment. Other feedback functions, initial states, alphabets, languages and boundaries remain open.",
  "next_steps": "A concrete feedback proposal should specify its recurrence, seed, boundary reset and output language before scoring; test matched planted examples and apply the same full selection to null controls.",
  "outcome": "not-supported",
